%T A400000 2,2,3,1,3,1,4,1,2,4,1,2,2,1,11,1,2,2,3,2,3,1,4,1,2,4,1,2,2,1,10,1,2,
%U A400000 2,3,1,3,1,4,1,2,4,1,2,2,1,11,1,2,2,3
%N A400000 The city-building sequence: a(n) = the height h of the shortest building which can see exactly h earlier buildings.
%C A400000 A building a(j) "sees" an earlier building a(i) if every intermediate building a(k), i < k < j, has its top (k, a(k)) on or below the straight line joining (i, a(i)) and (j, a(j)).
%C A400000 If at some point there doesn't exist a next height h at which the condition is satisfied, the sequence is finite. The sequence has at least 2.3 billion terms.
%C A400000 If the sequence is infinite, does every number eventually appear? The earliest appearance of height h is A399763(h). For record heights and when they appear, see A399949 and A399950.
%C A400000 The apparent "spikes" at n = 16k for k = 1..7 (10, 10, 11, 10, 11, 10, 19) follow a pattern similar to the values at n for n = 2..8 (1, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 3). Similar temporary analogies seem to emerge at various scales, but do any last indefinitely?
%C A400000 From _Rémy Sigrist_, Sep 16 2026: (Start)
%C A400000 The sequence is infinite: suppose that we have n > 0 terms:
%C A400000 - let v(k) be the number of buildings, among the first n buildings, visible from a building of height k >= 0 at position n+1,
%C A400000 - v(0) > 0 as we see the building at position n from the ground,
%C A400000 - v(k) = n for k sufficiently large as we see all the buildings from a point high enough,
%C A400000 - v(k) <= k for k sufficiently large,
%C A400000 - let h be the least k such that v(k) <= k (note that h > 0 as v(0) > 0),
%C A400000 - v(h-1) > h-1 by necessity; equivalently v(h-1) >= h,
%C A400000 - as v is weakly increasing, v(h) >= v(h-1) >= h,
%C A400000 - so v(h) = h, and a(n+1) = h.
%C A400000 The sequence b(n) = n-1 corresponds to the variant with the highest buildings.
%C A400000 The sequence is unbounded: by contradiction:
%C A400000 - boundedness implies a finite number of values,
%C A400000 - as the sequence is infinite, at least one value appears infinitely many times,
%C A400000 - let m be the greatest such value,
%C A400000 - a(n) <= m for n sufficiently large, say for n > s,
%C A400000 - the (m+2)-th occurrence of m beyond s sees the m+1 prior m's: a contradiction.
%C A400000 The k terms following a record high point, say a(n) >= k*(k-1), are independent of n: a(n+i) = a(i) + 1 for i = 1..k.
%C A400000 The sequence contains every integer: a(1) = 0; if v is a term, then v+1 is also a term.
%C A400000 Thanks to _Ivan N. Ianakiev_ for our discussions, which led to these comments.
%C A400000 (End)
%C A400000 a(n) is the backward degree of node n in the natural visibility graph of the sequence itself (Lacasa et al., 2008), with collinear points counted as visible: each new term is the least value equal to the number of earlier terms it is connected to. Consequently, Sum_{k=1..n} a(k) is the number of edges of the natural visibility graph of the first n terms, and the conjecture in the Formula section is equivalent to the asymptotic mean degree of this graph being at most 6. - _Francisco J. Muñoz_, Oct 26 2026
%C A400000 Before 1.48*10^11 no term exceeded 1998; afterwards there are 412 terms.  The distribution of heights is very stable: in the first 1.046*10^13 terms the frequencies of 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 are 31.13%, 32.76%, 15.68%, 9.31%, 4.28%.  The tallest building seen in the first 10^14 terms is 309063 at a(4961932825110). - _Jeff Sponaugle_, Oct 27 2026
